course: dictionary
lesson: 875
title: One word, two jobs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and bin is one of them. Read the words around it to find out which meaning the writer means. «Rubbish goes in the right bin» is about a container, because rubbish goes into it.
explain: Sometimes bin is an action word, a verb. «Dog poo can contain worm eggs, so it should always be picked up and binned.» Binned means thrown away. Swap in "thrown away" and the sentence still makes sense, so you know you have it right.
explain: Context can also show you which service is meant. «Council tax pays for local things like potholes and bins.» Bins here means the rubbish collection that the council provides. Ask yourself what would fit, then try your meaning in the sentence.
fact: Bin (noun) means a container for rubbish, or a container for storing things, like a bread bin.
fact: Bin (verb, informal UK) means to throw something away.
fact: «Binned» is the past form of the verb, so it means "was thrown away."
fact: In the UK, Australia and New Zealand people say bin; in the US they often say trash can or garbage can.
fact: Never climb into a bin or a skip. Broken glass goes in the bin carefully wrapped, and wash your hands after you take out the bin.
exercise: «Rubbish goes in the right bin: recycling, compost or general waste.» What is a bin in this sentence? | A loaf of bread | A tax taken from wages | A large box for a bath | *A container for rubbish
exercise: «Dog poo can contain worm eggs, so it should always be picked up and binned.» What does binned mean? | Kept safe for later | Washed clean | *Thrown away | Hidden under a bed
exercise: «Council tax pays for local things like potholes and bins.» What do bins mean here? | A tax taken from wages | A bread bin in the kitchen | *A rubbish collection paid for by the council | A storage box for toys
test: Jayden says, "I'll bin these old pens." What does bin mean? | *Throw away | Store in a box for later | Paint them bright red | Sell them at the market
test: «Wash your hands after you take out the bin.» Why is this important? | Bins must be washed in the sink | Bins are heavy, so your hands get tired | *Bins can carry germs, so clean hands keep you healthy | Hands always need washing anyway, so the bin does not matter
exam: «Cockatoos learned to flip open wheelie bins.» What is a wheelie bin? | A tax paid to the council | A bicycle with a big basket | A bag for carrying bread | *A large rubbish bin on wheels
